            <ArticleTitle>Paradoxic vocal fold movement disorder.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>135-46</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.otc.2013.08.014</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0030-6665(13)00117-5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Paradoxical Vocal Fold Movement Disorder (PVFMD) is a cause of dyspnea that can mimic or occur alongside asthma or other pulmonary disease. Treatment with Laryngeal Control Therapy is very effective once the entity is properly diagnosed and contributing comorbidities are managed appropriately. In understanding the etiology of PVFMD, focus has broadened beyond psychiatric factors alone to include the spectrum of laryngeal irritants (laryngopharyngeal reflux, allergic and sinus disease, sicca, and possibly obstructive sleep apnea). The following is a discussion of the history, terminology, epidemiology, diagnosis, comorbid conditions, and treatment of this entity.</AbstractText>
